2.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: The coat-symbolism you are referring to is a critical shorthand for Ochumelov’s chameleonic shifts of opinion and it is not a set of sustained physical descriptions in the text itself.

In the 1884 text Chekhov never gives a conventional descriptive portrait of Ochumelov’s outer garment at all.  The man is introduced as the police superintendent/petty official in uniform and the word *palto / шинель / overcoat* is only mentioned in passing, once or twice, as part of the stage business of the crowd scene. What the criticism conventionally calls “the coat” is the three / four stage-business moments in which Ochumelov adjusts his attitude to the dog – and by metonymy to his whole persona – in line with whom he thinks the dog belongs to:

* the confident, authoritarian arrival;
* the sudden timidity when the dog may be the General’s;
* the re-asserted authoritarianism when the dog is thought to be a stray;
* the final cringing retreat.

That is the three-shift pattern that the literature almost always quotes as the chameleon’s colour change, and it is that three-fold change of demeanour which is later read as being “buttoned/unbuttoned” in the coat. There is no separate, autonomous descriptive catalogue of the coat in Chekhov’s prose; the symbolism is imputed to the three moments of attitudinal change.

