12.text:
In the received Garnett / standard English tradition the garment is a running barometer not an inventory item.

There are three substantive, symbolically loaded re-descriptions of the шинель / overcoat, each of them mapping onto a change of colour in Ochumelov/Otchumyelov’s fearful mentality:

* entry / first display of petty official self-importance, the coat new / buttoned / ostentatious;
* the mid-scene nervous fiddling / buttoning / unbuttoning / warming of hands as he warms to the crowd and then defers to the idea of a gentleman’s dog;
* the final retreat / re-buttoning / cringing exit as the chameleon settles on the colour demanded by the imagined General.

That is the three-shift chameleon that criticism normally quotes. In the Garnett-type English text the lexical item overcoat / fur-coat / greatcoat is laid down four times – the neutral introductory naming plus the three symbolic re-colourings – and the Russian шинель is normally cited with essentially the same rhythm, three central descriptive occasions with a closing re-mention on exit. The core symbolic work is three distinct descriptive moments, conventionally rendered as four nominal mentions in translation.

